<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Ceiba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Ceiba is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Atlántida. It grew from the late nineteenth century as the base of the Vaccaro brothers' banana business, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-fruit-company">Standard Fruit Company</a>, whose railways and docks made it one of the country's main export ports. It is one of the largest cities in Honduras and a gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-ceiba.jpg" alt="La Ceiba" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: InterCoined · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letras_de_La_Ceiba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Ceiba lies on the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, forming part of the south-eastern boundary of the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. It is a municipality as well as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Atlántida. The city was named after a giant ceiba tree that grew near the old dock. It has a tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> climate, with an average annual rainfall of about 3,200 millimetres and a peak between October and February, when the trade winds are strongest. Pico Bonito, 2,435 metres high in the Nombre de Dios range, forms a backdrop to the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1872 Manuel Hernández built a small shack under the ceiba tree near the old docks. People then came from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Olancho and Santa Bárbara, and from around the world, drawn by work in the banana industry. La Ceiba was declared a municipality on 23 August 1877, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a> was president of Honduras. Late in the 19th century North American banana companies, among them the Vaccaro Brothers' Standard Fruit Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, took an interest in the trade, and their work attracted national and foreign immigrant labourers.</p><p>The neighbourhood called Barrio Inglés was the first recognised in the city and was named for its many English-speaking residents. The main thoroughfare was then the present Avenida La República, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-fruit-company">Standard Fruit Company</a> laid the railway that carried bananas from the plantations to ships at the port. The company was largely responsible for the early growth of the city. Banana and pineapple production formed the basis of the economy. Other enterprises followed, among them Banco Atlántida, founded in 1913 and described as the oldest bank in the country, and the brewing company Cervecería Hondureña, founded in 1918.</p><p>The first municipal building was made of wood and was burned in 1903 by people who wanted to destroy the records of private property ownership. It was set on fire again on 7 March 1914 during social unrest, and offices that had been moved were burned once more in 1924. Soon afterwards the present building was constructed on land donated by Manuel Mejía.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>La Ceiba has an estimated population of 209,000. It is the second most important port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cortes">Puerto Cortés</a>, and its pier offers ferry services to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a> and Útila. Each year the city holds a carnival in honour of its patron saint, Isidore the Laborer, on the third or fourth Saturday of May. Pineapple is the major export, and the Cangrejal River and the Cuero y Salado wildlife refuge lie close by.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Ceiba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The companies built up the ports and towns of <b>La Ceiba</b>, Tela and Puerto Cortés and helped make San Pedro Sula the commercial centre of the country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
